<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ntare V of Burundi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a>–<a href="/years/1972">1972</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Charles Ndizeye</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ntare V, born Charles Ndizeye, was the last king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a>. He deposed his father <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv">Mwambutsa IV</a> in July 1966 and was crowned, but in November of the same year his prime minister, Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a>, overthrew him and proclaimed a republic. Returning from exile in 1972, he was arrested and killed during the violence of that year.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ntare-v-of-burundi.jpg" alt="Ntare V of Burundi" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Associated Press · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ntare_V_of_Burundi_(1966).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Charles Ndizeye was born on 2 December 1947, the son of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mwambutsa-iv">Mwambutsa IV</a> and Queen Baramparaye Ruhasha, and was educated at the Institut Le Rosey in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>. His half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rwagasore">Prince Louis Rwagasore</a> was assassinated in 1961 while prime minister. After a Hutu-led coup attempt in October 1965 his father went into exile in Switzerland, leaving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> in effect without a head of state, while Tutsi officers under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michel-micombero">Michel Micombero</a> purged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> figures from the security forces and the political leadership.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>In March 1966 Mwambutsa named his only surviving son heir and entrusted him with overseeing the government. On 8 July Ndizeye dismissed Prime Minister Léopold Biha and suspended the constitution, and on 12 July Micombero presented a government with himself as prime minister and minister of defence. Ndizeye was crowned mwami on 3 September as Ntare V. Tension between the soldiers and the monarchy soon grew: on 7 November soldiers turned him away from the radio station when he tried to broadcast a decree dismissing the government, and on 28 November, while he was on a state visit to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a>, Micombero abolished the monarchy. His was the shortest rule of any Burundian mwami.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He lived in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-germany">West Germany</a> and later Uganda, and returned in March 1972 in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helicopter">helicopter</a> provided by the Ugandan president <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/idi-amin">Idi Amin</a>, who said Micombero had guaranteed in writing that he could live in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burundi">Burundi</a> as a private citizen. Within hours he was under house arrest in the former palace at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a>, and the government accused him of preparing a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mercenary">mercenary</a> invasion; whether he meant to return peacefully remains unclear.</p><p>On 29 April 1972, the day a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hutu">Hutu</a> uprising began, he was killed by soldiers in circumstances still unexplained. The government said he was shot trying to escape, while his supporters say he was shot by a firing squad and thrown into a common grave. The researcher Nigel Watt argued that the government feared monarchists would back the rebellion. Between 80,000 and 210,000 people died in the mass killings of Hutus that followed.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ntare V of Burundi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In November 1966 Captain Michel Micombero deposed the young king <b>Ntare V</b>, ending the Kingdom of Burundi and proclaiming a republic.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-burundi">Republic of Burundi</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gitega">Gitega</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/burundi">Burundi</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=-3.43,29.93&amp;year=1963" aria-label="Gitega on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5381 2292 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ntare V of Burundi"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5831 2562h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5831 2562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=-3.43,29.93&amp;year=1963">What was here in 1963? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
